name: the machine
boss: Spectrum
flavor: It is all in the air, and someone is always listening.
brief: Every machine will tell you what it is, if you ask. uname names the
brief: kernel, dmesg carries what the kernel said while booting, and /proc and
brief: /sys are the kernel's own filesystem -- files that are not on any disk.
brief:
brief: /proc is the habit worth forming. Nearly everything a monitoring tool
brief: reports, it read out of a file in there, and so can you.
---
q: {what kernel is this|which kernel is running|name the kernel this machine is running}
a: uname -s
out: Linux
ref: uname
teach: uname -s
hint: -s prints only the kernel name, the shortest of uname's fields
---
q: {everything uname knows, in one line|dump every field uname knows about, on one line|report the full uname summary in a single line}
a: uname -a
out: Linux
ref: uname
teach: uname -a
hint: -a strings together everything uname knows into one line
---
q: {just the kernel version|report only the kernel release version|what is the kernel version, and nothing else}
a: uname -r
out: [0-9]
ref: uname
teach: uname -r
hint: -r isolates just the kernel release/version number
---
q: {the machine's hardware architecture|what hardware architecture is this machine|report this machine's cpu architecture}
a: uname -m
out: x86_64|[a-z0-9_]+
ref: uname
teach: uname -m
hint: -m reports the cpu architecture uname was built for
---
q: {the network node name|what is this machine's network node name|report the node name uname reports}
a: uname -n
out: .
ref: uname
teach: uname -n
hint: -n is the node's network hostname, same field hostname reports
---
q: {the kernel build version string|report the kernel's build version string|what build string does the kernel report}
a: uname -v
out: .
ref: uname
teach: uname -v
hint: -v is the kernel's build string -- when and how it was compiled
---
q: {the operating system name|what operating system is this, according to uname|report the os name uname knows}
a: uname -o
out: .
ref: uname
teach: uname -o
hint: -o names the operating system, distinct from the kernel name
---
q: {the processor type|what processor type does uname report|report the processor type}
a: uname -p
out: .
ref: uname
teach: uname -p
hint: -p reports the processor type, when the kernel knows it
---
q: {the hardware platform|what hardware platform does uname report|report the machine's hardware platform}
a: uname -i
out: .
ref: uname
teach: uname -i
hint: -i reports the hardware platform, distinct from -m's architecture
---
q: {this machine's hostname|what is this machine's hostname|report the current hostname}
a: hostname
out: .
ref: hostname
teach: hostname
hint: hostname alone prints the current hostname
---
q: {the short hostname, without any domain part|just the hostname, with the domain part stripped off|report the hostname without its domain suffix}
a: hostname -s
out: .
ref: hostname
teach: hostname -s
hint: -s trims off everything after the first dot
---
q: {the fully qualified name including the domain|the hostname together with its domain, in full|report the fully qualified domain name}
a: hostname -f
out: .
ref: hostname
teach: hostname -f
hint: -f prints the fully qualified name, domain included
---
q: {just the dns domain part|only the dns domain, without the host|report just the domain part of the hostname}
a: hostname -d
ref: hostname
teach: hostname -d
hint: -d prints only the domain part, no host
---
q: {the ip addresses this host resolves to|which ip addresses does this hostname resolve to|report the ip addresses this host's name maps to}
a: hostname -i
ref: hostname
teach: hostname -i
hint: -i resolves the hostname to its ip addresses
---
q: {what the kernel said while it was booting|dump the kernel's boot-time messages|report everything the kernel logged while booting}
a: dmesg
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg
hint: dmesg alone dumps the whole kernel ring buffer
---
q: {kernel messages with the raw log level markers left in|dump the kernel log leaving the raw level prefixes in place|show kernel messages without stripping their raw level markers}
a: dmesg -r
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg -r
hint: -r keeps the raw <N> facility/level prefix on each line
---
q: {clear the kernel ring buffer after printing it|print the kernel ring buffer, then empty it|dump the kernel ring buffer and clear it once printed}
a: dmesg -c
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg -c
hint: -c prints the buffer then empties it
---
q: {set the console log level so only errors reach the screen|limit what reaches the console to error-level kernel messages|turn the console log level down to errors only}
a: dmesg -n 3
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg -n
hint: -n sets the console log level, not what dmesg itself prints
---
q: {read only the last 4096 bytes of the kernel buffer|dump just the final 4096 bytes of the kernel buffer|limit the kernel buffer read to its last 4096 bytes}
a: dmesg -s 4096
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg -s
hint: -s caps how many bytes of the buffer are read, from the end
---
q: %find% every line of the kernel log mentioning verity
a: dmesg | grep -i verity
ref: dmesg
teach: dmesg
hint: pipe dmesg into grep -i -- dmesg itself has no text filter
---
q: {what time is it|report the current date and time|what is the time right now}
a: date
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date
hint: date with no arguments prints the current date and time
---
q: {the time in utc rather than local time|report the current time in utc, not the local zone|what time is it in utc}
a: date -u
out: UTC|[0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -u
hint: -u reports the time in utc instead of the local zone
---
q: {today's date as YYYY-MM-DD and nothing else|report today's date as YYYY-MM-DD, no time attached|print only today's date, formatted YYYY-MM-DD}
a: date +%Y-%m-%d
out: ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$
ref: date
teach: date
hint: a +FORMAT argument controls exactly what date prints
---
q: {the current time as seconds since 1970|report now as a unix timestamp|what is the current time in seconds since the epoch}
a: date +%s
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: date
teach: date
hint: %s in a +FORMAT string is seconds since the epoch
---
q: {the date in iso 8601 form using the flag rather than a format string|today's date in iso 8601 form, via the dedicated flag|report the date in iso 8601 form without writing a format string}
a: date -I
out: ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$
ref: date
teach: date -I[SPEC]
hint: -I is a shortcut for iso 8601 output, no format string needed
---
q: {the date in rfc 2822 form, the one email headers use|report the date the way email headers format it, rfc 2822|print today's date in rfc 2822 form}
a: date -R
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -R
hint: -R formats the date the way rfc 2822 (email headers) expects
---
q: {the modification time of @txt@ rather than the time now|report @txt@'s modification time instead of the current time|when was @txt@ last modified, not what time is it now}
a: date -r @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: date
teach: date -r
hint: -r reports a file's modification time instead of the clock
---
q: {interpret a date string rather than reading the clock|parse a given date string instead of reading the system clock|turn a date string into a formatted date without touching the clock}
a: date -d 2024-03-01
out: 2024|Mar
ref: date
teach: date -d
hint: -d parses the string you give instead of reading the clock
---
q: {parse a date given in an explicit input format|parse a date string whose input format you specify yourself|interpret a date string using a format you name explicitly}
a: date -D "%Y-%m-%d" -d 2024-03-01 +%s
ref: date
teach: date -D
hint: -D names the format of the input date, paired with -d
---
q: {how long has this machine been up, straight from the kernel|report uptime straight from the kernel, not a wrapper around it|read this machine's uptime directly out of the kernel}
a: cat /proc/uptime
out: ^[0-9]
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: /proc holds files that are not on any disk
---
q: {what processor does this machine have, from /proc|report the processor details /proc knows about|read the processor information straight out of /proc}
a: cat /proc/cpuinfo
out: .
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: cpuinfo lists this the way /proc sees each core
---
q: {how much memory, from /proc|report memory usage as /proc sees it|how much memory does /proc report}
a: cat /proc/meminfo
out: Mem
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: meminfo is where free and top get their numbers too
---
q: {what command line was this kernel booted with|report the kernel's boot command line|which command line did this kernel boot with}
a: cat /proc/cmdline
out: .
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: cmdline is the exact string the bootloader handed the kernel
---
q: {what filesystems are mounted, read from /proc rather than asking mount|report the mounted filesystems by reading /proc, not the mount command|list what is mounted straight from /proc instead of asking mount}
a: cat /proc/mounts
out: /
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: mounts is the same list mount prints, straight from the kernel
---
q: {which terminal am i on|report which terminal device you are attached to|what terminal is this session on}
a: tty
out: .
ref: tty
teach: tty
hint: tty with no arguments prints the path of your controlling terminal
---
q: {test whether this is a terminal at all, silently|silently check whether this session is attached to a terminal|find out, with no output, whether stdin is a terminal}
a: tty -s
ref: tty
teach: tty -s
hint: -s suppresses output, useful only for its exit status
---
q: {clear the screen|wipe the terminal screen|blank the screen}
a: clear
ref: clear
teach: clear
hint: clear just writes the terminal escape sequence to wipe the screen
---
q: {put a terminal back to a sane state after a binary scrambled it|recover a terminal that some binary left in a broken state|reinitialize a terminal a misbehaving program has scrambled}
a: reset
ref: reset
teach: reset
hint: reset reinitializes the terminal, further than clear goes
---
q: %show% every terminal setting
a: stty -a
out: .
ref: stty
teach: stty -a
hint: -a dumps every setting, not just the summary
---
q: {dump terminal settings in the form you can restore later|save the terminal's settings in a form you can feed back in later|report terminal settings in the compact, restorable format}
a: stty -g
out: .
ref: stty
teach: stty -g
hint: -g prints settings in a compact form meant to be fed back to stty
---
q: {query the settings of a terminal other than your own|report the settings of a terminal that is not your controlling one|check another terminal device's settings, not your own}
a: stty -F /dev/tty1 -a
out: .
ref: stty
teach: stty -F
hint: -F points stty at a device other than your own controlling terminal
---
q: {rerun df every 2 seconds and watch it change|keep rerunning df every 2 seconds so you can watch it change|watch df's output update every 2 seconds}
a: watch df
ref: watch
teach: watch
hint: watch reruns a command every 2 seconds by default
---
q: {rerun df every 5 seconds instead|watch df, but on a 5 second interval instead|rerun df every 5 seconds}
a: watch -n 5 df
ref: watch
teach: watch -n
hint: -n sets the interval in seconds
---
q: {watch df with the header line suppressed|watch df but drop its header line|rerun df under watch with no header shown}
a: watch -t df
ref: watch
teach: watch -t
hint: -t hides watch's own header/title line
---
q: {wait two seconds and then continue|pause for two seconds before continuing|block for two seconds, then carry on}
a: sleep 2
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: sleep takes a number of seconds and blocks until they pass
---
q: %list% every pci device on this machine
a: lspci
ref: lspci
teach: lspci
hint: this is how you find out what network and storage hardware a strange box has
---
q: %list% pci devices showing which kernel driver is bound to each
a: lspci -k
ref: lspci
teach: lspci -k
hint: -k adds which kernel module claims each device
---
q: %list% pci devices in a form easy to parse
a: lspci -m
ref: lspci
teach: lspci -m
hint: -m switches to a machine-parseable, script-friendly format
---
q: %list% every usb device
a: lsusb
ref: lsusb
teach: lsusb
hint: lsusb walks the usb bus the way lspci walks pci
---
q: {write a line into the system log|log a line to the system log|send a line into the system log}
a: logger hello from xos
ref: logger
teach: logger
hint: logger writes its arguments as one line to the system log
---
q: {log a line tagged with a name of your choosing|log a line under a tag you choose|send a log line tagged with your own name}
a: logger -t xos investigating
ref: logger
teach: logger -t
hint: -t sets the tag that shows up in the log for this line
---
q: {log a line and also print it to your own terminal|log a line while also echoing it to your terminal|send a line to the log and print it locally too}
a: logger -s checking
ref: logger
teach: logger -s
hint: -s echoes the message to stderr as well as logging it
---
q: {log a line at a chosen priority|log a line tagged with a specific priority|send a log line at a priority you choose}
a: logger -p user.warn suspicious
ref: logger
teach: logger -p
hint: -p takes facility.level to set the message's priority
---
q: {open a serial console to a device at 115200 baud|connect to a serial console running at 115200 baud|dial into a serial device at 115200 baud}
a: microcom -s 115200 /dev/ttyS0
ref: microcom
teach: microcom -s
hint: when ethernet is dead, this is how you reach a router or another machine
---
q: {talk to a serial device giving up after 10 seconds of silence|open a serial device but bail out after 10 seconds of silence|connect to a serial device, timing out after 10 idle seconds}
a: microcom -t 10000 /dev/ttyS0
ref: microcom
teach: microcom -t
hint: -t sets an idle timeout in milliseconds before it gives up
